# Vercel Speed Insights Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js 14 (App Router) project.

## What Was Done

### 1. Documentation Review
- Fetched the latest installation instructions from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart
- Confirmed the framework-specific setup for Next.js 13.5+ with App Router

### 2. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0 using npm
- Package added to dependencies in package.json
- package-lock.json updated with new dependency tree

### 3. Code Configuration
**Modified File:** `app/layout.tsx`
- Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component in the body, placed after the existing `<Analytics />` component
- This placement follows the same pattern as the existing Vercel Analytics integration

### 4. Verification
✅ Build completed successfully (251 pages generated)
✅ Linter passed (only 1 pre-existing warning unrelated to changes)
✅ No tests to run (test script not configured in project)
✅ Lock files updated properly

## Implementation Details

The SpeedInsights component was added according to the official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router:
- Import from `@vercel/speed-insights/next` (framework-specific package)
- Placed at the root layout level for site-wide coverage
- No additional configuration required - the component works out of the box

## Next Steps for Deployment

After deploying this code to Vercel:
1. Enable Speed Insights in the Vercel dashboard (navigate to Speed Insights and click Enable)
2. The tracking script will be automatically injected after the next deployment
3. Monitor the Speed Insights dashboard after users visit the site to see performance metrics

## Files Modified
- `app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree

## Notes
- The implementation follows the same pattern as the existing Vercel Analytics integration
- SpeedInsights component is placed alongside Analytics for consistency
- No breaking changes introduced - all existing functionality preserved
- Build artifacts (.next/, node_modules/) were not staged as per best practices
